The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) is one of the SOLID principles in object-oriented programming. It states that objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of its subclasses without affecting the correctness of the program.

In other words, if we have a class hierarchy with a superclass `BaseClass` and a subclass `DerivedClass`, then any code that uses an instance of `BaseClass` should be able to work correctly with an instance of `DerivedClass` without knowing the difference between the two.

Here's an example to illustrate this principle:
```
// Base class: Animal
public abstract class Animal {
  public abstract void sound();
}

// Derived class: Dog
public class Dog extends Animal {
  @Override
  public void sound() {
    System.out.println("Woof!");
  }
}

// Derived class: Cat
public class Cat extends Animal {
  @Override
  public void sound() {
    System.out.println("Meow!");
  }
}

// Client code
public class Client {
  public void makeSound(Animal animal) {
    animal.sound();
  }
}

// Usage
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Client client = new Client();
    client.makeSound(new Dog()); // Output: Woof!
    client.makeSound(new Cat()); // Output: Meow!
  }
}
```
In this example, we have a base class `Animal` with an abstract method `sound()`. We have two derived classes `Dog` and `Cat` that extend `Animal` and override the `sound()` method.

The `Client` class uses the `makeSound()` method to make the animal sound. It takes an instance of `Animal` as a parameter, so it doesn't care whether the instance is a `Dog` or a `Cat`.

