To get user location permission in both Android and iOS apps developed using React Native, you can use the following common code snippet:
```javascript
import { PermissionsAndroid, PermissionsIOS } from 'react-native';

async function requestLocationPermission() {
  try {
    if (Platform.OS === 'android') {
      const granted = await PermissionsAndroid.request(
        PermissionsAndroid.PERMISSIONS.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION,
        {
          title: 'Location Permission',
          message: 'This app needs access to your location.',
          buttonNeutral: 'Ask Me Later',
          buttonNegative: 'Cancel',
          buttonPositive: 'OK',
        },
      );
      if (granted === PermissionsAndroid.RESULTS.GRANTED) {
        console.log('Location permission granted');
      } else {
        console.log('Location permission denied');
      }
    } else if (Platform.OS === 'ios') {
      const granted = await PermissionsIOS.requestPermission(
        PermissionsIOS.PERMISSIONS.LOCATION_WHEN_IN_USE,
      );
      if (granted === 'authorized') {
        console.log('Location permission granted');
      } else {
        console.log('Location permission denied');
      }
    }
  } catch (err) {
    console.warn(err);
  }
}
```
This code snippet uses the `PermissionsAndroid` module for Android and the `PermissionsIOS` module for iOS to request location permission from the user. It checks the current platform using the `Platform.OS` property and calls the appropriate method based on the platform.

On Android, it calls the `PermissionsAndroid.request` method and passes in the permission that we want to request (`ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` in this case), as well as an options object that contains the title and message that will be displayed to the user.

On iOS, it calls the `PermissionsIOS.requestPermission` method and passes in the permission that we want to request (`LOCATION_WHEN_IN_USE` in this case).

If the user grants permission, the `granted` variable will be set to `PermissionsAndroid.RESULTS.GRANTED` or `'authorized'`, and we can log a message to indicate that the permission has been granted. If the user denies permission, the `granted` variable will be set to `PermissionsAndroid.RESULTS.DENIED` or `'denied'`, and we can log a message to indicate that the permission has been denied.

If an error occurs during the permission request process, we catch it and log a warning message.

You can call this `requestLocationPermission` function wherever you need to request location permission in your React Native app. This code snippet should work for both Android and iOS platforms.

