#include <PyPortal.h>

PyPortal pyPortal;

void setup() {
  pyPortal.begin();
  pyPortal.setScreenOrientation(PORTRAIT);
  pyPortal.setTouchMode(TOUCH_MODE_INVERTED);
  pyPortal.setTouchThreshold(100);
}

void loop() {
  if (pyPortal.isTouched()) {
    int x = pyPortal.getTouchX();
    int y = pyPortal.getTouchY();
    int width = pyPortal.getTouchWidth();
    int height = pyPortal.getTouchHeight();

    if (x >= 0 && x < width && y >= 0 && y < height) {
      int touchCount = pyPortal.getTouchCount();
      if (touchCount == 2) {
        // Double tap detected!
        Serial.println("Double tap detected!");
      }
    }
  }
}
```
This sketch initializes the PyPortal touchscreen and sets the screen orientation to portrait mode. It then enters an infinite loop where it checks if the touchscreen is touched. If it is, it gets the current touch coordinates and width/height of the touch area. If the touch area is within the bounds of the screen, it checks if there are exactly two touches detected. If so, it prints a message to the serial monitor indicating that a double tap has been detected.

Note that this sketch assumes that the PyPortal touchscreen is connected to the Arduino board and that the PyPortal library is installed. You will need to adjust the code to fit your specific use case, such as changing the touch threshold or adjusting the touch area size.

Also, keep in mind that the PyPortal touchscreen has a limited number of capacitive touch points, so this sketch may not work reliably if you have multiple fingers touching the screen simultaneously. If you need to detect more complex touch events, you may want to consider using a different touchscreen library or hardware.
